Define relationships in each model’s `initialize()` method. The relationship methods accept:

1. Local field(s)
2. Referenced model
3. Referenced field(s)
4. Optional options such as `alias` and `reusable` [1][2]

### `belongsTo` — many-to-one

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model;

class InvoicesProducts extends Model
{
    public function initialize()
    {
        $this->belongsTo(
            'ixp_inv_id',
            Invoices::class,
            'inv_id',
            [
                'alias'    => 'invoice',
                'reusable' => true,
            ]
        );
    }
}
```

This means `InvoicesProducts.ixp_inv_id` references `Invoices.inv_id` [1][2].

### `hasOne` — one-to-one

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model;

class Invoices extends Model
{
    public function initialize()
    {
        $this->hasOne(
            'inv_cst_id',
            Customers::class,
            'cst_id',
            [
                'alias'    => 'customer',
                'reusable' => true,
            ]
        );
    }
}
```

This defines a one-to-one relationship from `Invoices.inv_cst_id` to `Customers.cst_id` [2].

### `hasMany` — one-to-many

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model;

class Invoices extends Model
{
    public function initialize()
    {
        $this->hasMany(
            'inv_id',
            InvoicesProducts::class,
            'ixp_inv_id',
            [
                'alias'    => 'invoiceProducts',
                'reusable' => true,
            ]
        );
    }
}
```

This means one `Invoices` record can have many `InvoicesProducts` records [1][2].

The fields may also be provided as arrays for relationships involving multiple fields. Phalcon also supports `hasManyToMany()` and `hasOneThrough()` for more complex relationships [1][2].

