Phalcon provides three hydration modes for model resultsets:

| Mode | Returned value |
|---|---|
| `Resultset::HYDRATE_ARRAYS` | Associative arrays |
| `Resultset::HYDRATE_OBJECTS` | Plain objects, typically `stdClass` |
| `Resultset::HYDRATE_RECORDS` | Model record objects |

`HYDRATE_RECORDS` is the default mode. These records remain connected to the ORM and can be modified and persisted using methods such as `save()`, `create()`, or `delete()`. Arrays and plain objects are read-only representations and are not connected to the database. [1]

### Change the mode on a resultset

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Resultset;

$invoices->setHydrateMode(Resultset::HYDRATE_ARRAYS);

foreach ($invoices as $invoice) {
    echo $invoice['inv_total'];
}

$invoices->setHydrateMode(Resultset::HYDRATE_OBJECTS);

foreach ($invoices as $invoice) {
    echo $invoice->inv_total;
}

$invoices->setHydrateMode(Resultset::HYDRATE_RECORDS);

foreach ($invoices as $invoice) {
    echo $invoice->inv_total;
}
```

The result types are implemented accordingly: simple resultsets hydrate records through model cloning, while complex resultsets create a model row, array, or `stdClass` based on the selected mode. [4][6]

### Specify hydration in a query

Hydration can also be passed through the `hydration` parameter of `find()`, `findFirst()`, and related finder methods:

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Resultset;

$invoices = Invoices::findFirst([
    'hydration'  => Resultset::HYDRATE_ARRAYS,
    'conditions' => 'inv_id = :id:',
    'bind'       => [
        'id' => 4,
    ],
]);

foreach ($invoices as $invoice) {
    echo $invoice['inv_total'];
}
```

Internally, `find()` reads this parameter and calls `setHydrateMode()` on the resultset. [1][5]

Eager loading requires `HYDRATE_RECORDS`; combining `eager` loading with `HYDRATE_ARRAYS` or `HYDRATE_OBJECTS` throws an unsupported eager hydration exception because those representations have no relation cache. [2]

