Use `Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Manager` to create a transaction, bind each participating model with `setTransaction()`, and explicitly `commit()` or `rollback()` the transaction.

```php
use MyApp\Models\Customers;
use MyApp\Models\Invoices;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Manager;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Failed;

try {
    $manager = new Manager();
    $transaction = $manager->get();

    $customer = new Customers();
    $customer->setTransaction($transaction);
    $customer->cst_name_last  = 'Vader';
    $customer->cst_name_first = 'Darth';

    if ($customer->save() === false) {
        $transaction->rollback('Cannot save customer');
    }

    $invoice = new Invoices();
    $invoice->setTransaction($transaction);
    $invoice->inv_cst_id = $customer->cst_id;
    $invoice->inv_total  = 100;

    if ($invoice->save() === false) {
        $transaction->rollback('Cannot save invoice');
    }

    $transaction->commit();
} catch (Failed $e) {
    echo 'ERROR: ', $e->getMessage();
}
```

Transactions make the SQL operations atomic: changes become permanent only when all operations succeed. If an operation fails, call `rollback()`; otherwise call `commit()` [1][2].

Finder or aggregate queries can also participate by passing the transaction using `Model::TRANSACTION_INDEX` in the query options [2]. Internally, model queries using the transaction obtain its connection, ensuring the transaction’s connection is used [4]. The manager can also manage multiple transactions and reuses an existing transaction through `getOrCreateTransaction()` [6].

